---
// Figure: a lock that expires mid-rebuild, released with DEL or with DELEX IFEQ.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './stampedes.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="st-hazard" title="When the lock expires before the rebuild ends" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. The timings (a 300 ms lock, a 400 ms query) were reproduced on Redis 8.10.3 with sleeps between commands; every reply shown was observed.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="release" label="A releases the lock with" value="del"
    options={[{ value: 'del', label: 'DEL' }, { value: 'delex', label: 'DELEX … IFEQ its token' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>In both versions, A takes the lock with a 300 ms expiry and starts a query that takes 400 ms. At 300 ms the lock expires while A is still working. B misses at 350 ms, finds no lock, takes it and starts a second query. At 400 ms A's query fails and A releases the lock.</p>
    <p>With DEL, A deletes the lock that now belongs to B. C then misses, finds no lock, takes one and starts a third query while B's is still running.</p>
    <p>With DELEX … IFEQ tok-A, Redis compares the lock's value with A's token, finds tok-B, deletes nothing and replies 0. C misses, finds B's lock, and waits. The short expiry still allowed two rebuilds at once, but the token check stopped a third.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { hazardSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="st-hazard"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, hazardSpecs, 'release'));
</script>
